# documentation unless they are meant for a model reading them. Extended
# regular expressions, matched case-insensitively, one per line; # starts a
# comment. Read by mh-sandbox-scan (grep -E) on the code before it runs, and
# by mh sandbox (Go regexp) on its output: write only what both understand —
# literals, ( | ), ?, +, *, [ ], no \b, no back-references.
#
# A signal, never a defence: base64, another language or a split phrase evade
# it. A match in an LLM application's own prompts is expected; read the line.
# Agent tooling (skills, prompts, injection tests) is full of these, so a
# phrase is graded warn; text hidden from a person (mh-sandbox-scan) is high.
# Each pattern earns its place by being rare in such repositories: no bare
# "system prompt", no "hey claude" (a skill's example request), no <system>.
